# Family Stuff

Both painful, and joyful.

## Family pains, new and old

TRIGGER WARNING: disturbingly violent threats (very tersely described), childhood trauma (largely undescribed). Skip to the “Family joys” section if you are at risk to such triggers.

Had a difficult conversation with my adult daughter, the middle of my three children, about trauma stemming from my parenting of her. Rough things to hear.

There were things she had to share that I cannot accept—that are decidedly not part of my reality. But too much of it rings unfortunately true. Much of it due to misconceptions, misunderstanding, and ignorance on my part. Ignorance of her autism, ignorance of the trauma that corporal punishment breeds (spankings, though they stopped when she was about 5 or 6). Especially, ignorance of what was going on in her head during her youth, including some misperceptions, distortions, and confusions between how I viewed patterns of behaviors and misbehaviors on her part, versus how I viewed her, as a person. There was apparently a deep-seated conviction, from around middle school, that I disapproved/judged her as a deeply-flawed person, and not merely disapproving of a season of poor choice-making.

My oldest child and I do not speak or have any form of contact. He had been my “best friend” and biggest fan, as he was growing up. As an adult, he dramatically rewrote our entire relationship history, and invented an assorted variety of abuses. He was also becoming just massively toxic in general: turning the most trivial differences of opinion into dramatic fights, mishearing siblings’ words as an attack and refusing to back down, making absolutely *everything* about him, and an increasingly violent default mentality. It began to affect everyone in the household, to the point where having him over for a visit required days of emotional loin-girding on everyone’s part, steeling themselves mentally for the coin-flip of whether it would be a day of exhausting dramatacisms, or a relatively peaceful visit (won mostly by walking on eggshells, and shutting the fuck up if I had a mild difference of opinion on anything, even something as trivial as fan lore for video games).

At one point, it reached a head. His personal attacks and general toxicity was reaching a point where it would genuinely take me multiple weeks to emotionally recover from encounters with him. It was jeapordizing my ability to function, at work and in life. It was also becoming clear to me that he had convinced himself that his rewritten narratives were truth, and that there was a profound disconnect between who he saw me as, and anyone I had ever been. (The gaslighting effect was a major component of his overall emotional effect on me.) I stared down the barrel of a lifetime of strained encounters, of ruined celebrations he was determined to make all about himself, of trying to counter the massive network of lies he’d surrounded himself with, and made a choice not to involve him in my life any longer.

The intent was not actually to completely sever contact, nor to “disown” him (as he proclaimed I’d done), nor even to stop supporting him financially. But his response (again in public Instagram posts), was to post profoundly disturbing, visceral fantasies and threats, of murdering me. Spoke of delighting/reveling as he’d watch the light leave my eyes. Of “opening” me with an axe.

So, yeah. Went full no-contact and never looked back. There was an email exchange about a year after.

It’s easy for me to dismiss his claims of abuse and trauma at my hands—it’s entirely based on fabricated stories, and also flies in the face of everything he’d ever said to everyone—his friends, extended family, therapists. He didn’t speak in carefully neutral tones, he practically worshipped me, including when he was alone in safe spaces, speaking to people he trusted and respected. The clear evidence is there, that his current history is an utter invention. (No, actually—it was _not_ easy, it was extremely painful and deeply confusing—but though I questioned myself repeatedly, there was never any real doubt as to what the truth actually was.)

It’s also impossible for me to entertain a history of having repeatedly put him down, judged and degraded him, when I spent his entire lifetime very much putting every ounce of energy I had, into the opposite of that. Into trying to combat his deeply warped and negative self-image, to make him understand how loved and cherished and wonderful he is, and that he doesn’t need to change anything at all about himself. The notion that I could have barraged him with personal attacks or had a pattern of saying anything to diminish him is genuinely ludicrous (and, as previously mentioned, flies in the face of how he characterized me during the actual years in question).

He has a Bipolar Type I diagnosis, with schizoaffective tendencies. As does the kids’ mother (also estranged, but not nearly to such a degree). They’ve both always had a... malleable... grasp on reality, very sensitive and vulnerable to influence, and (potentially) manipulation. I never thought it could be bent so far that it actually breaks so completely... but he had an experience (as an adult, away from me) that fundamentally broke him as a person, along with his sense of reality. Someone whom I’d thought to be a friend of mine also had a hand in directly manipulating his thoughts and reality.

It’s been a lot harder to hear accusations from his sister, though. Her sense of reality was never malleable in the way his was (except as a young child, and I somewhat suspect that may have had a role in forming some distorted impressions of events—but not as an adult, and never to the same degree). Distorted or not, some of what she points at as her trauma really happened, though much of it, I confess, I don’t understand how it could have produced trauma to the levels we spoke of. In some ways it doesn’t affect me in the way that her brother’s accusations did, largely because he wore some callouses into me... but her blows land, and linger, because they are steeped in truth. They may not be entirely made of truth, and a lot of it, as near as I can tell, is based in somewhat-distorted childhood impressions that were never communicated to me, out of an exaggerated fear of how I’d respond (built from those same impressions). I had no idea, and they were never brought to light (until now), and so instead of being able to address and comfort, they were allowed to fester and rot.

Like her brother, she accuses me of patterns of judgment and put-downs. As with him, these particular accusations, at least, are not true, and are the opposite of where I would mindfully put my energy... but it’s _closer_ to true. Because our relationship always had more friction, and especially during the middle-school years in which she formed negative impressions of me, we were butting heads a lot. She had patterns of a complete lack of impulse control, and I definitely felt overwhelmed. A lot of our relationship at that time was (appropriate) punishment—mostly, groundings. Restriction from access to electronics, restriction to staying in her room. There was yelling. As I say, I didn’t generally make it personal... but it’s easy to see there was opportunity to take things that way, opportunity for intent to be misinterpreted. And there were events when she was older that could have appeared to reinforce those impressions.

I learned, only from this recent conversation, of episodes of self-harm resulting from a combination of these (from my perspective) misunderstandings/misinterpretations, and the (more undeniably real) tendencies I’d have to express my anger, mainly through yelling.

We still have a relationship. It’s strained, and always has been. Partly because our personalities have just never meshed well, and partly, I’ve now learned, because her perception of me has been as an antagonist—an emotional threat, rather than a nurturer, and this has placed hard effective limits on what our relationship could be. Without knowing anything about everything she needed, needs, to heal from, I was completely unequipped and ignorant to do anything to aid her in doing so.

And it’s heartbreaking. It’s also clear to me—again only now—that she believes a lot more of her brother’s lies about our history than I’d understood, given how well some of it parallels her own experiences (formed when she actually _was_ still a child). They were discussing me, and their childhood, a lot in the time leading up to my decision to distance from the oldest, and in fact, just prior to the recent difficult discussion with the middle child, she’d recently visited with him. I suspect something had been stirred up in their recent visit, a bundle of emotions made raw that had previously been held more in check. I’m grateful for it, as I’m now finally made aware of things she’d previously withheld from me, things that I think are important for me to know about our relationship, and my standing with her, that I was largely (in some cases, completely) ignorant of (as bitter as they’ve been to learn). But I do also wonder, and worry, about what degree they may be helping each other to amplify or exaggerate their negative understandings of me, and their resulting trauma.

I know she doesn’t believe it all. Certainly not the more ridiculous accusations of direct abuse (things like throwing him into a dresser, or episodes of vaguely sexual creepiness (like, lingering hugs and hair-smelling (actually, he was the one who would awkwardly cling in hugs—and not only with me)). She’s certainly aware of his toxicity later on, as she was frequently the object of it. But the bits chorusing the pain she herself nurses regarding me.

It’s been slightly over a week since the conversation. I’ve been reeling, and processing. I have follow-up things to say to her—mainly, reiterating taking accountability for the things I am clearly responsible for, and to apologize for the pain she clearly is suffering from her past experiences with me—but also to gently reinforce that her brother’s false narratives really are false. But I’ve been careful not to be too quick to speak, and take my time contemplating how and what I wish to say.

She felt like our conversation (the hard one) went well, and told me it makes her feel positive about the future of our relationship. I was genuinely shocked to hear her say that, as quite a lot of the conversation consisted of her being very clearly distressed that I did not accept certain points of her narrative. But I guess it helped for her to get these things off her chest, and that I listened and didn’t react (even if I did flatly deny the reality of some things... while acknowledging that they reflect her genuine understanding and experience). I did also stop dead in my (conversation) tracks, took full responsibility and apologized for the use of spanking when she was young, and the role that played in her trauma.

I felt a lot less positive about the conversation, myself. But I suppose she was more prepared for what it would be than I was (I did know it would be rough, from experiences with previous conversations—just not how much).

It’s hard not to feel like a failure as a parent, with two of my three kids clearly feeling traumatized, and that I was a threatening figure to them (even if, for one of them, it’s due to genuine drangement/delusion, and not at all from reality). All I ever wanted to be was a safe space for my kids, for them to feel unambiguously loved, and that they could talk to me about anything. The oldest, the now psychotic one, is the only one who truly shared everything with me (not so much anymore). I have a good relationship with the youngest, and while he’s reasonably open about things, it’s never been radically so. Clearly, I was ignorant of how my daughter felt about me, for most of her entire life. I’m still very much recovering from that.

(If anyone’s wondering about psychiatric meds for my oldest kid, the estranged one: yes, he was on meds for most of his life. When, as an adult, he got diagnosed with autism, he decided we’d been attempting to “medicate his autism” all his life, and refused to continue with them, despite there clearly being quite a lot going on with him that is not explained by his ASD diagnosis alone. As an adult, and especially one not living with me, there really isn’t anything I can do about him no longer taking them. After I cut ties with him, he made Satan his surrogate father, and had already begun amassing a number of alternative identities, a “system” for his supposed dissociative identity disorder (DID). Some of these identities began life as separate from him, personifications/incarnations of his anxieties and dark impulses that he’d been encouraged to create by his therapist. He developed a deeply unhealthy, worshipful obsession with them, and eventually embraced them as alternative identities in a “system”. His relationship with these identities, and mass conversations they have with one another, and quick alternations within single online posts, do not resemble the behavior of other people I know with DID diagnoses. He does appear to me to be deeply psychotic, and there’s not really a damned thing I can do about it. Around the time I cut things off, he was also identifying as a “super-villain”. He is 26 years old. The severance of our relationship occurred four years ago next month. Anyway, if your reaction to some of what I’ve described is “It sure sounds like he needs to be medicated,” then yes, I agree, and no, it’s probably not going to happen. (And if it does, the damage to our relationship is already done.))

## Family Joys

In about a week, our household (myself, my wife (not the kids’ mother), and my youngest (19m)) are going on a road trip, to a family reunion in California. My parents, my seven younger siblings, and their families will be there.

My parents, and roughly half of us siblings, are Bible-literalist Evangelical Christians. My dad has been a pastor with Calvary Chapel my entire life. The oldest two brothers (myself and another) are atheists. Another brother is a Christian but with some very different theological beliefs than my parents. A sister is decidedly not Christian nor atheist, but would describe herself as “spiritual”. And another brother, I’m not entirely sure where his beliefs lie specifically, but he definitely has some trauma and resentment tied to his Christian upbringing.

And somehow we always get along amazingly well, when we all gather together. There is love, admiration, and mutual respect, and a genuine joy at being together. There are never any dramatics (at least, not unleashed before the group), no barely-contained seething, no passive-aggressive venting of unclosed wounds. No yelling. And always a generous/benevolent interpretation of anything said or done by other members of the family. Our get-togethers are typically relaxing, peaceful, and genuinely uplifting, throughout.

I have sometimes described our family dynamic as _weirdly_ functional. I’ve never been at any family gathering that gets along as well as ours _always_ does.

And, as mentioned, it’s not that the open wounds don’t exist. It’s not as if there isn’t any judgment about differences in belief, or concerns about who may or may not be in danger of hellfire upon their passing. Sometimes I wonder how much our remarkable peace may be upheld by a little too much restraint... but the truth is, it’s not like that. It’s not some façade that we barely maintain through tightly-gritted teeth. No one is being disingenuous about our true feelings toware one another. All our beefs have been communicated, nobody’s hiding it for the sake of (false) peace. We just take it as written that we know where we’re at, we don’t harp on things, and we don’t involve everyone else of the group with our grievances. Nor do we allow grievances, however legitimate, to prevent us from expressing and conveying genuine love and appreciation for each other.

There are times when, given my own dynamic with my kids, I feel the tiniest bit resentful, jealous, of how fantastially successful my parents were, using their Bible-based parenting philosophies that so frequently backfire on other parents, at fostering such a genuinely wholesome dynamic in my generation of the family, while our family dynamic (mine and my ex-wife’s, with our kids) often felt ugly. But I don’t really linger on it, and there’d be little point doing so. As successful (in this regard, at least) as my folks’ parenting style was, I could never have chosen to parent the same way they did. Allowing my kids the freedom to form their own conclusions about values and beliefs was important to me, and while I did use spanking when the two oldest kids were young, I fell out of using it before I had left Christianity, and was never very comfortable with it. Although I never felt traumatized by my parents’ use of corporal punishment, I know others, including some of my siblings, whose perspectives differ.

A couple of my siblings have chosen, at times, to distance from , or even go full no-contact for periods with, one or more of my parents. There were parenting choices they made, especially for some of the youngest of us, that had some damaging impacts.

But all of us, even those with a shakier history, acknowledge how truly blessed and fortunate we were to have the parents we did. Chief among our blessings, that we felt safe, physically and (mostly) emotionally in our homes, that we were enthusiastically encouraged in our pursuits and curiosity, that our parents modeled a very genuine love and support, especially for each other, throughout our upbringing, and that they modeled honest and open communication, and a philosophy of facing our hurts with honesty and in love, not suppressing, but also not unleashing.

I honestly view it as one of my superpowers, mine and my siblings’, that we had parents who were such excellent models of healthy communication styles, of sharing truth in love, of communicating things tactfully, and of listening quietly when someone has something real to say. That, and the sense of security and love that enabled me to put energy into learning and exploring things that have paid many dividends in my adult life and my career. And having been homeschooled, which, despite several important disadvantages, provided me the overwhelming advantage of fostering a love, and a thirst, for learning and constant drive for self-education.

And so I always look forward to seeing everyone, and spending time with them, every one of whom are genuinely amazing human beings, confident enough to thrive in being who they are, as varied as that may be, and confident enough, too, not to feel threatened by, or that we must compete with,  one another.

# Yet More Family Stuff

After the reunion, we’ll be dropping by to visit my ex-wife’s side of the family, whom my youngest son hasn’t seen in quite some time (nor the others that arent on this trip)—probably not since my divorce with his mother.

I fared rather well with her family during that time. There was a fairly obvious divide between the respect I was showing her, throughout, and the petulant tantrums and insanity she was throwing at me. Some of that wasn’t her fault, as she was withdrawing from some _grossly_ and prolongedly mis-prescribed medications for her mood disorders during that time. But some of it was because she had never really grown up, never learned to be a responsible adult. They could see how I treated her with compassion and respect, while at the same time drawing a line at how I would allow myself to be treated (culminating in the choice to divorce), and how she... didn’t (not toward me, nor towards them). And it won me points.

I mostly haven’t kept in touch with them. The occasional contact here or there, with years passing between. The main exception is my (ex) Mother-in-Law, who has made a point of reaching out, especially around Christmas time/my birthday, and also around my anniversary with my new wife. I appreciate her keeping the line open. I know it’s also with a view to keeping a path to a relationship with her grandkids.


